          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a dry cough for about ten days after a cold, no fever. Should I be doing anything?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in its red-flag list. That is not a clearance. If your symptoms change, see a clinician.\n\n**What the guidance says:** A cold usually lasts seven to ten days, and the cough often lasts two to three weeks after the other symptoms have gone. A dry cough at about ten days is within the expected range (Upper respiratory infection (common cold), General clinical reference). A cough that persists for a while is not, by itself, a sign of a bacterial infection or of treatment failure.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if you have:**\n- Shortness of breath, or difficulty speaking or breathing normally\n- Chest pain\n- Coughing up blood\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or fainting\n- A cough that gets clearly worse after you had been getting better. The influenza guidance describes this pattern as a possible sign of a secondary bacterial pneumonia, so it is worth prompt assessment.\n\nI could not retrieve guidance that specifically covers breathlessness, chest pain or coughing blood, so this list is a precaution rather than a sourced checklist.\n\n**Conditions a clinician might want to rule out:** The symptom grouping suggested the following, which are for discussion only and not a diagnosis:\n- Asthma. Cough alone, especially at night, can be an early presentation, and viral infections are a common trigger.\n- Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). This is relevant mainly with long-term smoking or other lung exposures.\n- Influenza. This usually comes with fever and body aches, which you have not reported, so it is less likely here.\n\nNote that the symptom tool listed \"fever\" because your description mentioned it. It does not mean you have a fever.\n\n**What you could do:** Book a routine appointment if the cough is not clearly easing, or if it has lasted about three weeks. Bring a short list of when it started, whether it is worse at night, and any smoking or asthma history. Seek care sooner if any of the warning signs above appear.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
